    "message": {
     "content": "Getting a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** without a competing written offer in hand is a major negotiation win. Google fully neutralized the $200,000 of Meta equity you would walk away from over the next 12 months.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Financial Breakdown\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Revised Google Offer (L6) | Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | +$12,000 |\n| **Equity (Annualized)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4) | +$20,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** | **+$200,000** |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$758,000+** | **+$232,000 (~44% jump)** |\n\n*(Note: If Google uses their standard front-loaded vesting schedule such as 33/33/22/12% or 38/32/20/10%, your Year 1 equity will be closer to $350k\u2013$410k, pushing Year 1 total comp above **$840,000**).*\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Multi-Year Comp Reality\n\n* **Year 1:** **~$758k \u2013 $840k+** (massive one-time liquidity boost).\n* **Year 2:** **~$560k \u2013 $620k** (base + bonus + grant + first year of stacked refreshes).\n* **Years 3\u20134:** **~$580k \u2013 $650k** (depending on how refreshers stack and performance ratings).\n\nFinancially, this is no longer a lateral move\u2014it provides substantial immediate upside and a solid baseline going forward.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Fine Print to Verify Before Signing\n\nBefore you say yes, clarify these three details with the recruiter:\n\n1. **Sign-On Payout & Clawback Terms:**\n   * Is the $200k paid in full on your first/second paycheck, or is it split across Year 1 and Year 2 (e.g., $100k/$100k)?\n   * What is the prorated clawback period if you leave voluntarily (typically 12 or 24 months)?\n2. **Equity Vesting Schedule:**\n   * Confirm the exact vesting cadence (e.g., standard 33%/33%/22%/12% front-loaded vs. standard 25% equal quarterly).\n3. **Start Date vs. Meta Vesting/Bonus Timing:**\n   * If you have a Meta vesting event or bonus payout within the next 3\u20136 weeks, align your Google start date to capture it before leaving.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Qualitative Comparison: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\nBecause you stated you were happy at Meta, comp is only half the equation:\n\n| Factor | Meta (IC6 PM) | Google (L6 PM) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Culture & Velocity** | High PM ownership, fast shipping, metrics/impact-driven. | Consensus-driven, engineering-heavy, higher review/launch overhead. |\n| **Work-Life Balance** | Generally more intense / performance-pressured (PSC). | Typically better WLB and stability (though varies by org). |\n| **Promotion Path** | Clearer path to IC7/Director if already high-performing. | Resetting your internal reputation; L6 $\\rightarrow$ L7 at Google can take time. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer, provided you are excited about the team/product scope.**\n\n* **Why take it:** You successfully forced them to match your base, increase your bonus target, and cover your 12-month unvested stock dollar-for-dollar with cash. A ~$230k+ Year 1 comp bump is rare at this level without an external bidding war.\n* **When to still decline:** Only if during interviews you got strong negative signals about the specific Google team's charter, or if you are on an active, near-term promotion track to IC7 at Meta that you do not want to reset.",
